From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: developerakshat12 <akafluidcore@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Use native Win32 CRITICAL_SECTION for recursive mutexes on Windows

On MinGW-w64 targets using libwinpthread, GCC libstdc++ defines
__GTHREAD_RECURSIVE_MUTEX_INIT, which causes std::recursive_mutex to
compile with a defaulted destructor (~recursive_mutex() = default;).
Consequently, pthread_mutex_destroy() is never invoked when a
std::recursive_mutex is destroyed.

Under winpthreads, pthread_mutex_lock dynamically allocates an internal
24-byte mutex handle structure on the heap on first lock. Because
pthread_mutex_destroy is omitted upon destruction, this 24-byte structure
is permanently orphaned on the process CRT heap.

In core PDF AST objects like Array, Dict, Annot, and Page—which are
instantiated and torn down by the hundreds of thousands during content
stream parsing, rendering, and text searching—this causes an unbounded,
reproducible memory leak (empirically measured at 5.08 MB per 892-page
search pass across 221,870 orphaned allocations).

This commit introduces poppler/PopplerMutex.h, providing a lightweight
cross-platform wrapper (PopplerRecursiveMutex). On Windows, it maps to
a native Win32 CRITICAL_SECTION initialized with a spin count of 4000
and explicitly cleaned up via DeleteCriticalSection in its destructor.
On other platforms, it aliases std::recursive_mutex with zero overhead.

Fixes severe memory leak on MinGW-w64 toolchains (e.g. MSYS2 UCRT64 / MINGW64)
introduced when recursive mutexes were added for thread-safety.
